<SiteLayout title="Offline-first approach — Retail POS Mobile" description="The planned offline-first architecture for Retail POS Mobile, including local work, queued sync, retries, and honest reconciliation of disconnected-device conflicts." path="/offline-first/">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / Offline-first</div><span class="eyebrow">A design constraint, not a slogan</span><h1>Offline should be explained—not oversold.</h1><p>Offline-first operation is a product goal. Business workflows are not yet available in a public release, so this page explains intended design principles rather than promising current behavior.</p><div class="status-line"><span class="status-pill status-planned"><span class="status-dot"></span> Planned architecture</span><span class="status-pill status-unavailable"><span class="status-dot"></span> Not publicly available</span></div></div></section>
<section class="content-section"><div class="container content-wrap"><h2>Three boundaries, each with a job</h2><div class="steps-grid"><article class="step-card"><span class="step-label">Step 01</span><h3>Work locally</h3><p>The planned local store is intended to keep supported lookups and queued work available when the network drops.</p></article><article class="step-card"><span class="step-label">Step 02</span><h3>Queue safely</h3><p>Important operations need durable identifiers and idempotent retries so a flaky connection does not create duplicate transactions.</p></article><article class="step-card"><span class="step-label">Step 03</span><h3>Reconcile on reconnect</h3><p>The cloud must validate incoming work and make conflicts visible rather than silently rewriting transaction history.</p></article></div>
<h2>The hard limit of multiple offline devices</h2><p>If two devices are disconnected from each other and both see the last two units of a product, neither device can know what the other sells while offline. No website promise or database trick can remove that information gap.</p><p>The intended approach is to keep local operations explicit, sync them safely, and surface inventory conflicts for manager review if they occur. The actual conflict policy must be implemented and tested before it is described as current capability.</p>
<div class="notice"><span class="notice-icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span><div><strong>What we will not claim</strong><p>We will not claim perfect shared stock awareness while devices are fully disconnected, or describe offline sales as released until the workflow has been validated.</p></div></div>
<h2>What needs testing before release</h2><ul><li>Connection loss during a transaction and during sync.</li><li>Retry behavior and duplicate prevention.</li><li>App restart while operations are waiting to sync.</li><li>Two devices selling limited stock while disconnected.</li><li>Conflict visibility and manager recovery steps.</li><li>Recovery after the network returns, including server-side validation.</li></ul></div></section></SiteLayout>
